- op: create  # e155
  at: '2026-06-07T03:16:06Z'  # e155
  by: agent  # e155
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e155
  clock: 1  # e155
  nonce: e155  # e155
  data:  # e155
    kind: work_item  # e155
    title: "bash I/O completely invisible to io-boundaries: no bash.yaml catalog + bash analyzer emits 0 external-command edges"  # e155
    status: todo_hard  # e155
    priority: 1  # e155
    parent:  # e155
    tags:  # e155
    - dogfood  # e155
    - dogfood_tranche_07  # e155
    isbefore: []  # e155
    duplicate_of: []  # e155
    not_duplicate_of: []  # e155
    pr_ref:  # e155
    description: "**Mechanism (two compounding gaps).** (1) There is NO bash.yaml (or csharp.yaml) in io_primitives/; the 14 catalogs are c/cpp/elixir/erlang/go/haskell/java/javascript/kotlin/objc/python/rust/scala/swift. bash/csharp have NO catalog at all (so they are not even part of WI-najil's 11/14 in_progress framing — they are 0/0). (2) The bash analyzer emits 291 calls edges but ALL target internal bash functions (run_test/_log/assert_exit/...); 0/291 target external shell commands (rm/curl/git/mkdir/...). So even with a catalog there would be no edge to classify.\n\n**Evidence.** io-boundaries --input substrate.json --json --include-tests (exit 0) reports unsupported_languages = [ansible, bash, csharp, html, json, markdown, solidity, sql, toml, wgsl] — bash/csharp are FIRST-CLASS analyzers (bash: 42 file nodes + 291 calls edges; 218 bash symbols) yet listed unsupported. io-boundary chains with a bash src = 0/25448.\n\n**Consequence.** The 42 analyzed bash files include .githooks/pre-push, scripts/auto-pr, scripts/ci-debug, scripts/ci-failover, scripts/contribute — scripts that git push, curl the Forgejo API (network), rm, write files, run subprocesses. A consumer auditing 'what touches network/fs/subprocess' via io-boundaries concludes these 42 scripts do NO I/O — substantively wrong. unsupported_languages honestly lists bash, but verify-claims must_not_exist (WI-kajil) does not consult it, so a boundary claim against a bash script still silently confirms.\n\n**Related (distinct).** Same class as WI-zavad (JS I/O invisible) but for bash (the most I/O-saturated language in this repo); distinct from WI-najil (the 14 catalog files' in_progress status, which doesn't cover bash/csharp's NO-catalog state). Source: dogfood_tranche_07 F132.A1, io-boundaries run against frozen substrate."  # e155
    fields: {}  # e155
- op: discuss  # dedb
  at: '2026-07-02T17:46:35Z'  # dedb
  by: agent  # dedb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dedb
  clock: 2  # dedb
  nonce: dedb  # dedb
  message: "SCOPED during a Wave-3 crush pass; NOT implemented — a blast-radius design point surfaced that makes this NOT a clean quick fix. The naive Part-A fix (add an else to bash.py's failed-resolver at ~448 emitting an unresolved calls edge for any command that resolves to neither a local nor a cross-file symbol) would FLOOD edge-count: bash's command grammar treats echo / printf / [ / test / cd / local / export / read / etc. (builtins) as commands, so ALL of them (not just curl/rm/git) would emit an edge on every bash-heavy repo — a bad corpus-wide change. The JS analogue (WI-zavad, done) avoided this by emitting only for a CURATED set (JS_KNOWN_GLOBAL_CALLS={fetch,...}), NOT every unresolved call. So WI-javoh needs GATED emission: emit the external-command edge only for known external I/O commands (net: curl/wget/nc/ssh/scp; fs_write: rm/mkdir/cp/mv/tee/dd/touch/rmdir; fs_read: cat/head/tail/grep; subprocess: git/docker/kubectl/systemctl), via a curated KNOWN_EXTERNAL_COMMANDS frozenset in bash.py mirroring the bash.yaml catalog. Verified mechanics (ready for implementation): io_boundary matches an edge via dst_ref.module_path == module_hint (io_boundary.py:1194) against the catalog module; io_primitives/bash.yaml should map module:<cmd> functions:[<cmd>] per boundary (mirroring javascript.yaml module:fetch functions:[fetch]). Part A = bash.py:448 gated else emitting Edge.create(dst=f'bash:{cmd}:0-0:{cmd}:unresolved', is_resolved=False, dst_ref=ExternalRef(lang='bash', module_path=cmd, name=cmd), meta call_locality=external_command). Part B = new io_primitives/bash.yaml. Still needs awaits_bakeoff_validation (new edges corpus-wide even when gated). The curated-command-set design + catalog sync is the real work."  # dedb
- op: discuss  # 401d
  at: '2026-07-05T00:06:43Z'  # 401d
  by: agent  # 401d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 401d
  clock: 3  # 401d
  nonce: 401d  # 401d
  message: "Re-scoped via ruling A (decision recorded in ADR-0016 implementation-note, dev c3a2f6df45; CODE pending). Investigation: bash external commands are NOT an io-boundary catalog gap — a bash script shelling out to curl/rm/git is a 'subprocess' boundary (launching an external program, ADR-0016's own definition), and the invoked program's I/O is opaque (Opaque tier, is_resolved=False; no in-tree source, no transitive funnel). DROP the bash.yaml net/fs catalog idea (it would mis-attribute curl's network activity to the shell script — a conceptual leak). Emit a 'subprocess' edge for any command that is neither an in-repo function nor a shell BUILTIN (closed denylist = principled stopping point; uniform subprocess classification removes the curated-allow-list treadmill). subprocess is default-high-risk via the existing invariant, so command risk needs no new taxonomy. STAYS todo_hard: the bash.py emission code (fill the empty-else at ~bash.py:458 with is_resolved=False subprocess edges) is unwritten. Separate independent fix: Erlang os:cmd is mis-homed under env_read/env_write in erlang.yaml."  # 401d
- op: discuss  # 105c
  at: '2026-07-05T11:15:58Z'  # 105c
  by: agent  # 105c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 105c
  clock: 4  # 105c
  nonce: 105c  # 105c
  message: "Design + adversarial-review workflow (5 agents; full analysis: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/design_analyses/wi-javoh-bash-subprocess-io-design_07052026.json) surfaced a REFUTATION OF RULING A's gate. Mechanism is settled: Design 1 — bash.py's implicit-else (external command, not a local fn, resolver-miss) emits ONE 'calls' edge (is_resolved=False, dst_ref=ExternalRef(bash,cmd,cmd)) that DIRECTLY prestamps edge.meta['io_boundary']='subprocess'; compute_boundary_map's aggregation loop (io_boundary.py:1320-1355) already turns any meta.io_boundary edge into a real subprocess IoChain (the same Path A python subprocess.run rides), so NO io_boundary.py consumer change, is_resolved never consulted, and F3 stays intact (the edge skips Path B at line 1172 because io_boundary is set). Contract-safe: 'subprocess' already in CATALOG_BOUNDARY_TYPES, no schema bump, is_resolved=False is the only FK-legal cell for an external_symbol dst. BUT BOTH designs rated 'floods': ruling A's CLOSED shell-builtin DENYLIST is the WRONG POLARITY — it blocks only ~48-70 builtins, while the subprocess flood is external TOOLS (git/grep/sed/awk/cat/find/head/dirname/…), none of which are builtins. Empirical: the workers ran the exact emission over hypergumbo's OWN ~29 shell scripts -> ~700-823 low-signal subprocess edges (e.g. 171 identical git chains from one file), which would drown curated python/go/rust subprocess entries AND inflate the F3-protected total_io_edges headline; a real CI/devops repo yields thousands. REQUIRED REVISION (contradicts ruling A): (1) replace the closed denylist with a POSITIVE gate — an ALLOWLIST of I/O-relevant external launches (curl/wget/git/docker/ssh/scp/rsync/kubectl/aws/gcloud/psql/mysql/nc/openssl/gpg/package-install verbs) OR the HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES set (rm/curl/kubectl/terraform/docker/ssh/…), so the bucket carries signal not census; (2) dedup to ONE edge per (caller, command_name). Needs human ruling: ruling A's 'closed shell-builtin-denylist, every external command -> subprocess' is empirically a flood; adopt the positive-allowlist/high-risk revision, or route bash externals to the EXCLUDED external_potential bucket instead of the counted real category. Setting needs_human_review."  # 105c
